        <div id="isolate-vs-blend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Isopure vs Gold Standard: Is 100% Isolate Worth It Over a Blend?
          </h2>

          <p>
            100% isolate is worth paying for if carbohydrate, fat or lactose matters to you, and it is not worth paying for if you simply want 24 to 25 g of protein at the lowest price. Isopure Zero Carb lists whey protein isolate as its only protein, per the Vitacost label. Gold Standard lists a protein blend of whey protein isolate, whey protein concentrate and hydrolyzed whey protein, in that order.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ingredients are listed by weight, so isolate is the largest of Gold Standard's three proteins. That is why we describe it as an isolate-led blend rather than a concentrate. Optimum Nutrition does not publish the ratio, though, so nobody outside the company can tell you how much concentrate is in a scoop. What the label does show is the result: 3 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and 1.5 g of fat per serving in Double Rich Chocolate, against 0 g, 0 g and 0.5 g for Isopure Zero Carb.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolate is whey that has been filtered further to strip out most of the fat and milk sugar that concentrate keeps. The trade-off is cost: the extra processing means isolate is usually priced higher per gram, and Isopure follows that pattern. For the protein itself there is little to separate them. Both are complete dairy proteins, and neither label breaks out leucine, the amino acid most linked to muscle protein synthesis; Optimum Nutrition only states 5.5 g of BCAAs per serving on its brand page.
          </p>

          <p>
            The hydrolyzed whey in Gold Standard is a small selling point rather than a deciding one. Hydrolyzed protein is partly pre-digested, which brands market as faster absorbing, but it sits third on the list and its share is not disclosed. If you want a powder that is all isolate and all hydrolyzed, that is a different product: see our explainer on <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> for how the three forms differ and when each makes sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule: if you are bulking, eating carbs anyway and tolerate milk fine, the blend loses you nothing and saves you money. If you are on a ketogenic or very low-carb plan, logging every gram, or know that regular milk upsets you, the isolate is the safer pick.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lactose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Is Better for Lactose Intolerance and Low-Carb Diets?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Zero Carb is the better choice for lactose intolerance and for low-carb diets, because it lists 0 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar per scoop. Lactose is a milk sugar, so it is counted inside a label's sugar and carbohydrate figures. A powder that lists 0 g of sugar cannot be carrying much lactose; Gold Standard lists 1 g of sugar per serving, and some of that could be lactose from the concentrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither brand publishes a lactose figure, so this is an inference from the label rather than a measured number. US labeling rules let a brand round any amount under 0.5 g down to zero, which means Isopure's 0 g of sugar is best read as "under half a gram", not "none". Isopure Zero Carb is not marketed as lactose-free, and it is still a dairy protein, so it is unsuitable for anyone with a milk allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            For mild lactose intolerance the difference between the two may not matter. Many people who react to a cup of milk, whose label shows about 12 g of sugar that is almost all lactose, are fine with a gram or less. The people most likely to notice are those who react to small amounts, or who take two or three scoops a day, where Gold Standard's sugar adds up to 2 to 3 g. If you are unsure, try a small tub of Gold Standard before switching to the pricier isolate; if it bothers you, Isopure is the logical next step.
          </p>

          <p>
            On carbs, Isopure Zero Carb is the only one of the three with a flat zero, which makes it easy to log on a keto plan. Gold Standard's 3 g per serving is small, and much of it comes from the cocoa in Double Rich Chocolate rather than the whey, but it still counts toward a 20 to 30 g daily carb ceiling. Isopure Low Carb sits between them at 1 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more option for the lactose-sensitive: Isopure's clear protein, sold on the brand store as Isopure Clear Protein and listed in our database under its old Infusions name, is described as lactose-free in its Amazon listing title, though the brand page itself does not make that claim. It is a juice-style drink rather than a shake, with 20 g of protein per serving. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">best lactose-free protein powder</a> guide covers powders that do carry the claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
