          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition is cheaper: Gold Standard costs about $1.44 per 25 g of protein against about $2.04 for Isopure Zero Carb, so Isopure costs roughly 42% more per gram. Those figures use the prices in our database, recorded on 1 November 2026: $79.99 for a 58-serving, 3.96 lb tub of Gold Standard at Target and $89.79 for a 44-serving, 3 lb tub of Isopure Zero Carb at Vitacost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over a month the gap becomes real money. Someone taking 50 g of protein a day from powder would spend about $86 a month on Gold Standard and about $122 on Isopure Zero Carb at those prices, a difference of roughly $36 a month or more than $400 a year. Isopure Low Carb, at $98.03 for about 41 servings, works out to about $2.39 per 25 g, which is the most expensive of the three for the same protein.
