          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing a small tub of one with a big tub of the other</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gold Standard's 1.98 lb tub on the brand site worked out to about $2.68 per 25 g, more than Isopure's 3 lb tub. Its 3.96 lb tub was about $1.44. Compare the same size class, or better, divide each price by its total grams of protein before deciding which brand is cheaper.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Isopure is lactose-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isopure Zero Carb lists 0 g of sugar, which under US rounding rules means under half a gram, not none. It is not sold as lactose-free and still contains milk protein. It suits most people with mild lactose intolerance, but it is not safe for a milk allergy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating every Gold Standard flavor as NSF certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Only Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream appeared in the NSF Certified for Sport database when we checked, out of 24 US flavors. Certificates apply to specific products. If you are drug-tested, buy a listed flavor and look up the lot number before you open the tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying isolate prices when the blend would do</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you eat carbs freely and milk does not bother you, Gold Standard's extra 3 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of fat make no practical difference. Paying roughly $36 a month more for Isopure, at 50 g of powder protein a day, buys you nothing you would notice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading an old label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gold Standard panels differ between lots: one current listing shows a 31 g scoop and 130 mg of sodium, older ones show 30.4 g and 50 mg. Isopure's flavors differ too. Check the supplement facts on the tub you buy rather than a review or a cached product photo.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Isopure better than Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb is better for carbs and lactose: 25 g of protein with 0 g of carbohydrate and sugar, against 3 g and 1 g for Gold Standard. Optimum Nutrition is better on price and verification: about $1.44 per 25 g versus $2.04, plus an NSF Certified for Sport listing. For most people Gold Standard is the better overall buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which has more protein, Isopure or Gold Standard?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb has slightly more: 25 g per 31 g scoop against 24 g per 31 g scoop for Gold Standard 100% Whey, per the labels we recorded. That makes Isopure 81% protein by weight and Gold Standard 77%. Over a day the 1 g difference is trivial; carbohydrate, fat and price separate them far more than protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard vs Isopure Low Carb: which is better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Gold Standard is the better value against Isopure Low Carb. It costs about $1.44 per 25 g against $2.39, carries an NSF certification on two flavors and scores 78/100 to Low Carb's 55. Isopure Low Carb only wins on fat and carbs, at 1 g of each, and on being a pure isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Isopure or Gold Standard better for lactose intolerance?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb is the safer choice, because it is pure whey isolate and lists 0 g of sugar, where lactose would show up. Gold Standard contains whey concentrate and lists 1 g of sugar per scoop. Neither brand publishes a lactose figure or sells these tubs as lactose-free, and neither suits a milk allergy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Isopure third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We could not find an independent certification for Isopure. A search of the NSF Certified for Sport database for Isopure returned no results on 20 November 2026, and the product listings we read claim none. Gold Standard 100% Whey was listed by NSF in Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream when we checked.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which is better for keto, Isopure or Optimum Nutrition?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb is the better keto fit, with 0 g of carbohydrate, 0 g of sugar and 0.5 g of fat per scoop on its label. Gold Standard adds 3 g of carbohydrate per serving in Double Rich Chocolate, which is 6 g a day on two scoops. That is manageable on most low-carb plans but tight on a strict 20 g limit.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Gold Standard whey isolate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Gold Standard 100% Whey is a blend of whey protein isolate, whey protein concentrate and hydrolyzed whey protein, with isolate listed first and therefore the largest share by weight. Optimum Nutrition does not publish the ratio. The brand sells a separate Gold Standard 100% Isolate for buyers who want isolate only.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

